  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "Full paper text supplied, including appendices.",
    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "Compares SAM, random perturbation (RandomSAM), and VariationalSAM and reports CIFAR test accuracies; this is a different method and measure bearing on optimizer/regularization effects on generalization. It does not test memorization of random labels or the claim’s central capacity result.",
    "gate_note": "Table 3 and Table 4 report test accuracies for optimizer variants. The text also gives theoretical analyses and cites prior generalization results, but cited results and theoretical bounds are not empirical rows. Training accuracies are not reported.",
    "test_reason": "The study compares sharpness-seeking training algorithms and derives their implicit regularization properties, but tests none of V1–V8. In particular, it does not measure magnitude-aware sharpness, training–test gaps, or their ranking correlation as V7 requires. ASAM is not an Adam-versus-SGD comparison, and two WideResNet widths do not establish V2's behavior beyond interpolation.",
    "authors_conclusion": "The record accurately captures the authors' assessment that VariationalSAM is unpromising, RandomSAM approaches SAM on CIFAR-10, and limited tuning constrains the comparison. Qualify the claim that SAM performs best: it leads all Table 3 comparisons, but Table 4 contains higher ASAM/FSAM point estimates in some settings. The authors also emphasize their theoretical connection between SAM and variational inference.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "Compares SAM with random Gaussian perturbation and learned-covariance VariationalSAM on CIFAR-10/100; these experiments do not match the claim’s interventions or outcomes.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Dataset, WideResNet size, optimizer, and epoch allocation; RandomSAM is close to SAM on CIFAR-10 but has a larger performance gap on CIFAR-100.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "SAM has the highest accuracy among the four Table 3 methods in every dataset–architecture combination. RandomSAM nearly matches it on CIFAR-10 but trails it more on CIFAR-100; VariationalSAM is worse.",
        "proposed_claim": "Worst-case perturbation training outperforms the tested random and learned-covariance variants under these recipes, with a small advantage over random perturbations on CIFAR-10.",
